\subsection{Similarity}{

\code{similarity} holds the psychophysical similarity of an option to the
target as a function of their distance on the wheel, one value per
distance in option steps, in the order 0, 1, ..., \code{n_options / 2}: the
first value belongs to the target itself and must be 1, the last to the
option opposite the target. The model assumes the similarity depends only
on the distance, the same for every target and on both sides of it. The
values are fixed, not estimated; Schurgin et al. (2020) measured them in
a separate perceptual matching task. With 360 options in 1-degree steps,
\code{similarity[k + 1]} is the similarity at \code{k} degrees. Without a measured
similarity, \code{\link[=tcc_similarity]{tcc_similarity()}} returns a von Mises or Laplace shape for a
chosen \code{kappa}; the fit is then a TCC with that shape fixed. A model that
estimates the width of the gradient from the responses is the \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}.
}

\subsection{Noise}{

\code{noise} sets the distribution of the noise on each option's signal.
\code{"gaussian"}, the default, is the model of Schurgin et al. (2020), with
normal noise of unit variance. Its response probabilities have no closed
form, so on the full wheel the likelihood reads them from a table (see
the section Grid limit on d); with \code{options} it integrates them per
cell. With \code{"gumbel"} the noise follows the standard Gumbel (type I
extreme value) distribution. The largest of independent Gumbel
variables is again Gumbel distributed, which turns the probability of
choosing an option into the softmax of \code{d * f(x)} over all options,
\verb{exp(d * f(x)) / sum(exp(d * f(x')))} (Oberauer, 2023, eq. 4). This needs
no table, so \code{d} has no grid limit and \code{d_max} is not used.

Gumbel noise is the first step on Oberauer's (2023) path from the TCC to
his signal discrimination model, and it keeps the similarity measured and
fixed. Estimating the width of the similarity gradient is the next step,
and that model is the \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}. The two meet at a von Mises similarity:
with \code{similarity = tcc_similarity("vonmises", kappa)} and \code{noise = "gumbel"}, \code{\link[=dtcc]{dtcc()}} is the \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} density at each option's centre times
the option width \code{2 * pi / n_options}, with the same \code{kappa} and \code{c = d * sqrt(2 * pi / kappa) / (1 - exp(-2 * kappa))} in the default
parametrization of \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}. The sum over options stands in for the
integral of the \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}, so the two agree to 1e-8 in log probability
while the gradient's SD, \code{1 / sqrt(kappa)} radians, spans about 4 options
or more (\code{kappa} up to 200 on 360 options), and differ by 0.013 at \code{kappa = 1000}.

Per gradient, Gumbel noise is not cheaper than normal noise: with \code{d} per
subject and set size on \link{oberauer_lin_2017} it cost 1.5 times as much.
It skips the table build before sampling and has no grid limit. Keep
normal noise to stay with the model of Schurgin et al. (2020) and to
compare \code{d} with their d'. The two \code{d} are not on one scale. The
standard Gumbel distribution has an SD of \code{pi / sqrt(6)} = 1.28, but
matching the response probabilities takes a larger factor: the Gumbel
\code{d} with the smallest Kullback-Leibler divergence from the probabilities
of the normal-noise model was 2.6 to 2.9 times the normal \code{d} for values
of the latter from 0.5 to 2, and 1.9 to 2.4 times at 8, for Laplace
similarities with \code{kappa} 2.9 and 1.7 and a von Mises similarity with
\code{kappa} 4.
}

\subsection{Response}{

With \code{units = "radians"}, \code{resp_error} is the response minus the target
in radians, as for \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} and \code{\link[=mixture2p]{mixture2p()}}, and each value is assigned to
the nearest of the \code{n_options} options (+-pi fall into the same option).
With \code{units = "bins"}, it is the chosen option's offset from the target in
option steps. The model treats the response as a choice among
\code{n_options} discrete options, so \code{n_options} should be the number of
options the participant could choose from.

\code{n_options} is part of the model, not a resolution setting. Each option
draws its own noise, and the more options lie near the target, the more
often one of them wins: at \code{d = 2}, with the Laplace similarity of the
examples built for each wheel (\code{tcc_similarity(..., n_options = n)}), the
probability of choosing an option at most 10 degrees from the target is
0.67 on 360 options, 0.82 on 3,600 and 0.89 on 36,000. The model has no
continuous limit, and values of \code{d} fitted with different \code{n_options} are
not on one scale.
}

\subsection{Grid limit on d}{

\code{d} is modelled on the log scale, so it is positive, effects on it are
multiplicative, and priors on its coefficients are priors on \code{log(d)}.
Keep those priors proper: as \code{d} approaches 0 the likelihood levels off
at that of guessing uniformly, so under a flat prior on \code{log(d)} the
posterior cannot be normalised, and a cell with little information
drifts towards \code{d = 0} without end. The default prior is proper. This
holds for either noise; the rest of this section applies to normal noise
on the full wheel only, not with \code{options}. The likelihood reads the
response probabilities from a table on a grid of \code{d} values up to
\code{d_max} in steps of 0.02, built once per chain
by quadrature and interpolated with a cubic spline (error below 1e-6 in
log probability). Beyond \code{d_max} the table is extended
linearly, with the log probability of each response allowed to fall but
not to rise. This keeps the sampler moving during warmup but is not the
model: past \code{d_max} the likelihood no longer favours a larger \code{d}, so a
posterior that reaches the end of the grid follows the prior beyond it,
with estimates that mean nothing. \code{bmm()} warns after the fit when any
posterior draw of \code{d} passes \code{d_max}; then refit with a larger \code{d_max}.
The default of 30 lies far above the values of \code{d} from 0.6 to 2.0 that
\code{tcc()} estimates for set sizes 1 to 8 in \link{oberauer_lin_2017} with the
similarity of the examples below (\code{d} depends on the similarity). Each
extra unit adds 50 table rows, which costs build time and memory but
nothing per gradient. A large \code{d} is weakly identified
when the similarity is broad, because the probability of reporting the
target then grows slowly with \code{d}.
}

\subsection{Posterior predictive checks}{

\code{pp_check(fit)} compares observed and predicted offsets in option steps.
\code{pp_check(fit, resp_var = "error")} shows the same on the scale of
\code{resp_error}, which for radians is the view \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} and \code{\link[=mixture2p]{mixture2p()}} fits
give; add \code{type = "bars_binned"} and name a condition column in \code{group}
for histograms per condition. \code{resp_var = "abs_error"} shows the distance from the target,
the quantity the likelihood depends on. See \code{\link[=pp_check.bmmfit]{pp_check.bmmfit()}}.
\code{posterior_predict()} and \code{predict()} return offsets in option steps
whatever \code{units} is; multiply by \code{2 * pi / n_options} for radians.
}

\subsection{Comparing with continuous models}{

The log-likelihood of \code{tcc()} is a log probability mass over options,
whereas \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} and \code{\link[=mixture2p]{mixture2p()}} return a log density in radians. The two
differ by the log of the option width, \code{log(2 * pi / n_options)} per
observation, so summed over \code{N} observations by \code{N * log(2 * pi / n_options)}; for 360 options, \code{log(2 * pi / 360)} is -4.048. Before
comparing LOO estimates, convert the TCC log-likelihood into a density:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{ll <- brms::log_lik(fit_tcc) + log(n_options / (2 * pi))
loo_tcc <- loo::loo(ll, r_eff = loo::relative_eff(exp(ll), chain_id =
  rep(seq_len(brms::nchains(fit_tcc)), each = brms::ndraws(fit_tcc) /
  brms::nchains(fit_tcc))))
loo::loo_compare(loo_tcc, brms::loo(fit_sdm))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{loo_compare()} warns that the models do not share the same y variable,
because \code{tcc()} stores the binned response; the warning is expected here.
The comparison is fair only if the responses were themselves recorded on
\code{n_options} discrete options, as on a 360-degree colour wheel.
}

\subsection{Speed}{

The fit is fastest when rows that share a value of \code{d} are adjacent, so
bmm offers to sort the data by the predictors of \code{d}, as for \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}.
Continuous predictors of \code{d} are supported at the per-trial cost.
}

\subsection{Forced-choice option sets}{

With \code{options}, each trial offers only some of the \code{n_options} positions,
as in an m-alternative forced choice, and the response is the offered
option with the strongest signal: the probabilities above are taken over
the offered options only. The columns named in \code{options} hold the offered
options of each row, relative to the target and in the units of
\code{resp_error}, with \code{NA} where a row offers fewer options than there are
columns. They must include the target (an offset of 0) and the response,
and must sit on the grid of \code{n_options} positions (values within a tenth
of a step are rounded onto it). Sets may differ in size and spacing from
trial to trial; rows that offer the same options, in any order, share
one set. A data set with two- and four-option trials on a 360-option
wheel, in degrees for readability (convert them with \code{\link[=deg2rad]{deg2rad()}}):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{err  opt1 opt2 opt3 opt4
  0     0  180   NA   NA
 90     0   90  180  -90
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

fitted with \code{tcc("err", similarity, options = paste0("opt", 1:4))}.
When every trial offers \code{m} options spaced equally around the wheel from
the target, \code{n_alternatives = m} builds these columns; with \code{options},
\code{n_alternatives} may instead name a column holding the number of options
of each row, which is checked against the option columns. The data
contract is the one of \code{sdm(options = )}, so the same data fit both models.

The design is that of Robinson et al. (2023), who offered 2, 4, 8 or 16
equally spaced colours (16 options lie 22.5 degrees apart, so they need
\code{n_options = 720}). Their critical test fixes the parameter across m and
compares the two noises: with one value for all m, the normal-noise TCC
fitted better than the softmax (Gumbel) model, while both fitted alike
with the parameter free per m. In bmm the test compares \code{d ~ 1} under
\code{noise = "gaussian"} with \code{d ~ 1} under \code{noise = "gumbel"}, on the same
similarity and \code{options}. The Gumbel fit is their softmax model, and both
log-likelihoods are log probabilities over the offered options, so
\code{loo_compare()} reads them as they are (see the examples). Comparing
\code{d ~ 1} with \code{d ~ 0 + m_condition} within one noise checks whether that
model needs a parameter per m. \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} with the same \code{options} gives this
check for \code{c} (\code{c ~ 1} against \code{c ~ 0 + m_condition}); with \code{kappa}
estimated it is not their softmax model, which keeps the measured
similarity. \code{d} under normal noise and the Gumbel parameters (\code{d} with
\code{noise = "gumbel"}, or \code{c} of \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}}) are not on one scale (see the
section Noise), so compare the fits, or how each parameter varies across
m, not their values.
For either noise, the log-likelihood is a log probability over the
offered options, as for the full wheel (see the section Comparing with
continuous models); \code{sdm(options = )} reports it on the density scale,
so add \code{log(n_options / (2 * pi))} per observation before comparing a
\code{tcc()} fit with an \code{\link[=sdm]{sdm()}} fit.

Under normal noise the likelihood integrates over the offered options
once per cell, a combination of a value of \code{d} and an option set, and
needs no table, so \code{d_max} is not used. Measured on one chain and one
thread with fixed-step NUTS on an Apple M3 Max laptop, a cell cost about
0.07 ms per gradient with 2 options and 0.18 ms with 16. The design of
Robinson et al. with \code{d} per participant and m (30 participants, 15,000
trials, 120 cells) took 14 ms per gradient, and 500 trials with random
foils, each trial its own cell, took 41 ms with 4 options and 87 ms
with 16. Gumbel noise needs no integral and took 1 ms and 0.2 to 0.3 ms
for the same designs. \code{bmm()} says so when there are more cells than half
the rows, as with a continuous predictor of \code{d} or foils drawn anew on
each trial. \code{log_lik()}, \code{posterior_predict()} and \code{pp_check()} read each
row's options from the data; to predict, pass \code{newdata} that carries the
option columns and a response that is one of the offered options (any
one will do, as \code{posterior_predict()} ignores it). \code{log_lik()} and \code{loo()} evaluate each
row on its own under normal noise, unless consecutive rows share their
draws and options; for 4,000 draws on the same laptop this took 0.13 s
per row with 4 options and 0.38 s with 16.
}
}
\examples{
\dontshow{if (isTRUE(Sys.getenv("BMM_EXAMPLES"))) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
f <- tcc_similarity("laplace", kappa = 1 / deg2rad(20))
dat <- data.frame(err = rtcc(1000, d = 2, similarity = f))
fit <- bmm(bmf(d ~ 1), dat, tcc("err", similarity = f), cores = 4)

# Gumbel noise with a fixed von Mises similarity
f_vm <- tcc_similarity("vonmises", kappa = 4)
fit <- bmm(bmf(d ~ 1), dat, tcc("err", similarity = f_vm, noise = "gumbel"), cores = 4)

# Forced choice among 2, 4, 8 or 16 equally spaced options (Robinson et al.,
# 2023), 125 trials each, on a wheel of 720 half-degree positions
f720 <- tcc_similarity("vonmises", kappa = 4, n_options = 720)
m <- c(2, 4, 8, 16)
afc <- data.frame(m_condition = factor(rep(m, each = 125)))
offered <- lapply(m, function(k) (seq_len(k) - 1) * 2 * pi / k)
afc[paste0("opt", 1:16)] <- t(vapply(rep(offered, each = 125), function(x) {
  c(x, rep(NA, 16 - length(x)))
}, numeric(16)))
afc$err <- unlist(lapply(offered, function(x) {
  rtcc(125, d = 1.8, similarity = f720, n_options = 720, options = x)
}))
tcc_afc <- tcc("err", similarity = f720, n_options = 720, options = paste0("opt", 1:16))
fit_d1 <- bmm(bmf(d ~ 1), afc, tcc_afc, cores = 4)
fit_dm <- bmm(bmf(d ~ 0 + m_condition), afc, tcc_afc, cores = 4)
tcc_gumbel <- tcc("err", similarity = f720, n_options = 720, options = paste0("opt", 1:16),
                  noise = "gumbel")
fit_g1 <- bmm(bmf(d ~ 1), afc, tcc_gumbel, cores = 4)
# Robinson et al.'s critical test: one d for all m, normal against Gumbel noise
loo::loo_compare(brms::loo(fit_d1), brms::loo(fit_g1))
sdm_afc <- sdm("err", n_options = 720, options = paste0("opt", 1:16))
fit_c1 <- bmm(bmf(c ~ 1, kappa ~ 1), afc, sdm_afc, cores = 4)
fit_cm <- bmm(bmf(c ~ 0 + m_condition, kappa ~ 1), afc, sdm_afc, cores = 4)
# within one model, does one parameter hold across m? Here the data come
# from one d, so the normal-noise model should need no d per m
loo::loo_compare(brms::loo(fit_d1), brms::loo(fit_dm))
loo::loo_compare(brms::loo(fit_c1), brms::loo(fit_cm))
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
